  "body": "GPU stock sim S2 (#2627), a part of the fix of the slow stock simulation (#3336).\n\n## What changed\n\n- `crates/gpu/src/cut.wgsl` (new): the removal kernel. One workgroup (8 x 8) for each work item (one chord against one 8^3 brick). Each invocation cuts the eight z cells of one column. A claim folds into the lattice with `atomicMax` on the order-preserving u32 key of the S1 twin. Three entry points in one compute pass:\n  - `cs_fill` writes the `-4 cap` floor key.\n  - `cs_cut` claims.\n  - `cs_decode` turns each key into the bits of its f32, in place. The buffer is then the f32 layer in the `pack_cut` layout (x fastest, then y, then z).\n- `crates/gpu/src/cut.rs` (new): the plain-value input (`CutInput`, `CutGrid`, `CutItem`), its validation, and `cut_cpu`. `cut_cpu` copies the f32 twin of camcraft-sim operation for operation.\n- `crates/gpu/src/cut_gpu.rs` (new): `CutKernel` (three pipelines, compiled one time for each device), the buffer plan against the device limits, at most 256 items in one dispatch of `cs_cut`, and a read-back with a time bound (`CUT_WAIT` = 10 s on the poll, 100 ms on the map callback).\n- `crates/gpu/src/cut_run.rs` (new): the fallback rule `cut_or_cpu` / `cut_or_cpu_on`:\n  - A missing device, a kernel error or a timeout gives the CPU law, with the reason logged one time.\n  - Each GPU batch gets a spot check: up to 64 texels are compared with the CPU law. A GPU value more than half of the claim margin away rejects the batch.\n  - A timeout or a failed spot check turns the GPU path off for the process.\n- `crates/sim/src/stock_sim/cut_twin_work.rs`: the new `twin_items` gives the kernel items as plain numbers (`TwinItem`). `run_twin` now runs over those items. The result of `run_twin` does not change (the 7 old tests of `stock_cut_twin` stay green).\n- `crates/sim/tests/stock_cut_twin.rs`: the new test `the_gpu_kernel_fixtures_match_the_twin` writes and checks `crates/gpu/tests/fixtures/cut_twin_{ball,flat}_2627.txt` (`CAMCRAFT_BLESS=1` writes them again, the `facet_grid_2619` pattern). Each fixture has the items, an FNV-1a hash of the twin layer, and the f64 layer (sparse). Sizes: 114 KB and 158 KB.\n- `docs/decisions/2627-gpu-removal-kernel.md`: the decisions.\n\nNo new crate edge. `camcraft-gpu` keeps no `camcraft-*` dependency. The issue text asked for a `camcraft-gpu -> camcraft-sim` edge. The kernel needs only numbers, so the caller passes them in (decision file). No new external dependency. No change to `Cargo.toml` or `Cargo.lock`.\n\nDisplay only: no verdict, post gate or posted G-code reads this kernel. `simulate_document` does not change.\n\n## Readings (this machine, adapter \"Microsoft Basic Render Driver\" via Dx12, the software adapter)\n\nParity, f32 GPU against the f64 field cut (one-sided law of `stock_cut_twin::parity`, tolerance `96 * f32::EPSILON * 64 + 1e-4` = 8.3e-4, not changed):\n\n| Batch | Items | GPU above f64 | Largest lag under f64 | max abs(GPU - CPU twin) | Smallest claim margin |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| fixture, ball | 132 | never | 1.03e-4 | 0 (bit equal) | 4.9e-5 |\n| fixture, flat | 224 | never | 1.54e-4 | 0 (bit equal) | 8.9e-5 |\n| synthetic, ball (96x96x32) | 1536 | - | - | 0 (bit equal) | 1.1e-4 |\n| synthetic, flat (96x96x32) | 1152 | - | - | 0 (bit equal) | 2.0e-4 |\n\nTwo runs on one device are bit equal. The CPU law in camcraft-gpu is bit equal to the sim twin (hash check).\n\nTimings, release build, one batch (`cut_parity`, `--release`):\n\n| Batch | GPU (software adapter) | CPU law (one thread) |\n|---|---|---|\n| synthetic ball, 1536 items, 166740 claimed texels | 20.3 ms | 17.5 ms |\n| synthetic flat, 1152 items, 165415 claimed texels | 30.4 ms | 157.4 ms |\n| fixture ball, 132 items | 11.9 ms | 1.1 ms |\n| fixture flat, 224 items | 3.8 ms | 8.1 ms |\n\nThe kernel build (three pipelines) takes 4.2 ms. The GPU times include the upload, the three passes and the read-back.\n\n## Pinning tests\n\n- `crates/gpu/tests/cut_parity.rs` (6 tests):\n  - `the_cpu_law_answers_the_sim_twin_bit_for_bit`\n  - `the_gpu_cut_never_removes_more_than_the_f64_cut_when_an_adapter_exists`\n  - `one_larger_batch_agrees_with_the_cpu_law_and_is_timed`\n  - `no_device_falls_back_to_the_cpu_law_with_its_reason`\n  - `a_layer_off_the_cpu_law_fails_the_spot_check`\n  - `bad_input_refuses_by_name`\n- `crates/sim/tests/stock_cut_twin.rs`: `the_gpu_kernel_fixtures_match_the_twin` (red first: the fixture did not exist, then blessed).\n\nWithout an adapter, the two GPU tests print `SKIPPED ... NOT a pass` and check that the CPU law answers. With `CAMCRAFT_REQUIRE_GPU=1` they fail. With `CAMCRAFT_GPU_COMPUTE=off` they skip by name (run: 6 passed). No test opens a window.\n\n## Gate\n\nSteps that I ran on head 5eb67ba20 (debug, `-j 6`):\n\n- `gpu build`, `gpu suites: lib` (6), `bin_parity` (3), `cut_parity` (6), `refusals` (6): green.\n- `sim build`, `sim suites: lib` and each sim integration suite of the quick plan, `stock_cut_twin` (8) included: green.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-gpu -p camcraft-sim --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n\nOwed to the trial gate (in the quick plan, not run here by the instruction of the coordinator):\n\n- `motion build`, `motion suites: lib`, `motion suites: posed_subtract`.\n- `app build` and the 70 `app suites` steps of `cargo xtask gate quick --base origin/main --dry-run`.\n\nHeavy lanes reached (`cargo xtask gate heavy --base origin/main --dry-run`): `app heavy` (206 steps) and `motion heavy` (37 steps). Not run. \"Not run\" is not a pass.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- A hardware adapter. `device_async` takes the software adapter first (#2621, item 4), so the readings are from WARP only. FMA contraction or a different `sqrt` on a hardware adapter can move the GPU value. The claim margin and the spot check are there for that case.\n- The timeout path on a real hang (no test makes a device hang). The timeout text and the latch are in `cut_run.rs`.\n- The viewport wiring. That is S3 (#2629). Two facts for S3 are on #2629:\n  - The shared device of `camcraft-gpu` is not the render device of the UI (`crates/ui/src/trace.rs:197-203`). The caller of #2629 makes its own `CutKernel` on the render device, or reads back and uploads with `write_cut`.\n  - The kernel writes u32 ordered keys during the cut. `cs_decode` turns them into f32 bits in the same buffer, so `trace.wgsl` can read the buffer as `array<f32>` after the pass.\n\n## Issues filed\n\n- #3529 (P7): the binning read-back of `binning_gpu.rs` waits with no time limit.\n\nChangelog: The stock simulation gets a GPU removal kernel for ball and flat end mills on linear moves, with a CPU fallback (not wired to the viewport yet).\n\nEffort: max\n\nRefs #3336\n\nFixes #2627\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
